  'praktik_3.5': <ArticleBlock>[
    Heading('The 5 Daily Prayers: When and How Many Rakaat?'),
    Paragraph('You now know HOW to pray. The next question: WHEN exactly, and how many rakaat for each time?'),
    Subheading('⏰ The 5 Obligatory Prayer Times'),
    Paragraph('• Fajr/Subuh: from true dawn until sunrise. 2 rakaat.'),
    Paragraph('• Dhuhr/Zuhur: when the sun has passed its zenith (around 12:00). 4 rakaat.'),
    Paragraph('• Asr: when an object\'s shadow equals its length (around 15:00). 4 rakaat.'),
    Paragraph('• Maghrib: right after sunset until the red sky fades. 3 rakaat.'),
    Paragraph('• Isha: after Maghrib until true dawn. 4 rakaat.'),
    Highlight('Fajr = 2, Dhuhr = 4, Asr = 4, Maghrib = 3, Isha = 4. Total: 17 rakaat a day. Only 17 — it\'s not much when spread across the day.'),
    Subheading('🤔 Why Do the Rakaat Differ?'),
    Paragraph('The number of rakaat was set directly by Prophet Muhammad ﷺ based on revelation. Fajr is only 2 because its window is short (dawn to sunrise). Maghrib is 3 as a "transition" between day and night. Dhuhr, Asr, and Isha are each 4 because their windows are longer.'),
    Paragraph('There\'s no "why" that can be fully explained by logic — this is a decree from Allah through His Prophet. What\'s clear: every rakaa count carries its own wisdom.'),
    Subheading('📱 Want to Start Tracking?'),
    Paragraph('If you\'re ready to start the 5 daily prayers, open the Home tab in this app. There you can track your daily prayers, see prayer times for your city, and collect XP every time you pray!'),
    Cta('Now you know the schedule! Take the quiz, then start praying from the easiest one (Fajr). 🎯'),
  ],
  'praktik_3.6': <ArticleBlock>[
    Heading('Things That Confuse Beginners'),
    Paragraph('This is the final module! We\'ll answer the questions beginners ask most. If you have one that isn\'t here, ask someone who understands better or a nearby ustadz.'),
    Subheading('❓ "What if I forget which rakaat I\'m at?"'),
    Paragraph('Totally normal, especially at the beginning. The solution: take the smaller number. If unsure between rakaat 2 or 3, just assume you\'re at rakaat 2. Add 1 rakaat, then sit for the final tasyahud + salam. After the salam, do sujud sahwi (2 extra prostrations) as compensation for the slip.'),
    Highlight('Forgetting is human. No panic. Take the smaller count, add sujud sahwi. Your prayer remains valid.'),
    Subheading('❓ "What if I don\'t know the long recitations?"'),
    Paragraph('No problem! What\'s OBLIGATORY is Al-Fatihah in every rakaat. The short surah after it? You may recite ANY surah you know — Al-Ikhlas, An-Nas, Al-Falaq, Al-Kautsar, even Ayat Kursi. Your choice.'),
    Paragraph('If you haven\'t memorized any short surah at all? Focus on Al-Fatihah first — that\'s the most obligatory. The short surah can come gradually. The Prophet ﷺ himself said: "Recite whatever is easy for you."'),
    Subheading('❓ "What are jamak and qashar?"'),
    Paragraph('These are special concessions for someone traveling (musafir):'),
    Paragraph('• JAMAK — combining two prayers into one time. Jamak taqdim: Dhuhr + Asr at Dhuhr time. Jamak takhir: Maghrib + Isha at Isha time. Jamak also happens at Arafah (Dhuhr + Asr) and Muzdalifah (Maghrib + Isha) during hajj.'),
    Paragraph('• QASHAR — shortening a 4-rakaat prayer into 2. Allowed for Dhuhr, Asr, and Isha. The condition: a musafir (long-distance travel, usually more than 80 km).'),
    Paragraph('Jamak and qashar can be combined. So Dhuhr + Asr can be both jamak and qashar into 2 + 2 rakaat at one time. It\'s a concession from Allah — Islam doesn\'t overburden.'),
    Subheading('❓ "Can I pray in Indonesian?"'),
    Paragraph('This question comes up a lot. The answer:'),
    Paragraph('The pillars\' recitations (Al-Fatihah, takbir, ruku, sujud, tasyahud, salam) MUST be in Arabic. Why? Because the Quran was revealed in Arabic, and prayer is worship already standardized by the Prophet ﷺ. Changing the pillars\' recitations = changing worship already established.'),
    Paragraph('BUT additional duas outside the pillars (like the dua after the final tasyahud, before salam) may use ANY language, including Indonesian. So you can pour your heart out in your own language after the obligatory recitations.'),
    Highlight('Pillars\' recitations = Arabic (obligatory). Additional duas = any language. This keeps your prayer standardized yet heartfelt in your own words.'),
    Subheading('❓ "My prayer isn\'t perfect — is it valid?"'),
    Paragraph('If the validity conditions are met and the pillars are present, it\'s valid. No need for perfection. The Prophet ﷺ himself taught new converts gradually. Allah knows you\'re still learning.'),
    Paragraph('What matters: DON\'T STOP. An imperfect prayer is better than no prayer at all. Over time it gets smoother. Everyone starts from zero.'),
    Subheading('🎉 Congratulations! You\'ve Finished Every Category!'),
    Paragraph('From "Why believe in God?" to "Things that confuse beginners" — you\'ve passed 19 learning modules!'),
    Cta('Alhamdulillah, all modules complete! 🎉 Now open the Home tab and start tracking your prayers. You\'re on the right path! 🚀'),
  ],
  'rukun_2.1': <ArticleBlock>[
    Heading('The 5 Pillars of Islam: A Muslim\'s Foundation'),
    Paragraph('Okay, now that you understand the fundamentals of belief (\'Aqidah), the next question is: once you believe, then what? The answer is the Pillars of Islam.'),
    Paragraph('The 5 Pillars of Islam are the things that form the FOUNDATION of a Muslim\'s life. "Pillar" means a supporting column — remove one and the structure shakes. The five complete one another; you can\'t just pick and choose.'),
    Subheading('1️⃣ Shahada: "I Bear Witness"'),
    Paragraph('This is the gateway into Islam. The two sentences of shahada: witnessing that there is no god but Allah, and that Muhammad ﷺ is His messenger. It\'s not only spoken but believed in the heart. It\'s a lifetime commitment, not just words.'),
    Highlight('The shahada is like "terms & conditions" — but ones you actually read and agree to, not just hitting "accept."'),
    Subheading('2️⃣ Salat: Five Times a Day'),
    Paragraph('Salat is the way to converse directly with Allah, 5 times a day. Fajr, Dhuhr, Asr, Maghrib, Isha. Not an empty ritual — there\'s movement, recitation, and meaning in every step. It\'s a standing appointment with God. It cannot be delegated, and it cannot be skipped.'),
    Subheading('3️⃣ Zakat: Giving from Wealth'),
    Paragraph('Zakat is not a tax — it\'s a purifier. The concept: your wealth isn\'t 100% yours; others have a right to a share of it.'),
    Subheading('4️⃣ Fasting: A Full Month'),
    Paragraph('Every Ramadan, Muslims fast from sunrise to sunset. It\'s not just holding back from food and drink, but also holding back emotions, gossip, and negativity. The goal: to train discipline, empathy for those in need, and closeness to Allah. One full month, every year.'),
    Subheading('5️⃣ Hajj: Once in a Lifetime'),
    Paragraph('Hajj is obligatory once in a lifetime for those who are financially and physically able. Millions gather in one place, and not a single person stands out by wealth or status.'),
    Subheading('💡 Why 5, Not 3 or 7?'),
    Paragraph('These five pillars were established directly by Prophet Muhammad ﷺ. Each fills a different aspect of life: Shahada = the heart, Salat = time, Zakat = wealth, Fasting = desires, Hajj = the body. Complete — no more, no less.'),
    Paragraph('In the following modules, we\'ll go through them one by one in detail, starting with the Shahada in the next module. Stay tuned!'),
    Cta('You\'ve got the overview! Now take the quiz to claim your XP. 🎯'),
  ],
  'rukun_2.2': <ArticleBlock>[
    Heading('Shahada: The First Gateway'),
    Paragraph('"Laa ilaaha illallah, Muhammadur Rasulullah." You\'ve definitely heard this sentence. But what does it actually mean? And why is it the first pillar?'),
    Paragraph('The shahada isn\'t a magic chant. Not a talisman. It\'s a declaration — the official statement from your heart that you\'re choosing a certain path in life. Let\'s break it down.'),
    Subheading('📜 The First Sentence: Laa Ilaaha Illallah'),
    Paragraph('"There is no god (right of worship) except Allah." This is the core of Tawhid we covered in module 1.2. Not merely saying "God exists," but also "He alone is worthy of my worship and obedience."'),
    Paragraph('The implication: you must not worship anything besides Allah. You must not fear anything more than Him. You must not depend on anything more than Him. This is about life\'s priorities.'),
    Highlight('Laa ilaaha illallah = "Allah is number one in my life. Not money, not position, not other people."'),
    Subheading('📜 The Second Sentence: Muhammadur Rasulullah'),
    Paragraph('"Muhammad ﷺ is the messenger of Allah." This means you believe Muhammad ﷺ truly was sent by Allah as a life example. And if you believe that, the consequence: follow his teachings.'),
    Paragraph('Imagine: you have a mentor proven to be honest, wise, and caring. You trust him. So you follow his advice — that\'s logical, right? Same with Muhammad ﷺ: if you truly believe he\'s God\'s messenger, follow his teachings.'),
    Subheading('⚡ The Logical Consequences'),
    Paragraph('The shahada isn\'t just speech — it\'s commitment. Once you say it and believe it, the logical consequences follow:'),
    Paragraph('• You commit to worshiping Allah alone: praying, making dua, giving thanks — all directed to Allah.'),
    Paragraph('• You commit to following the Prophet\'s ﷺ teachings — the way of life he demonstrated.'),
    Paragraph('• You commit to leaving what\'s forbidden — not out of fear of punishment, but because you believe Allah knows best for you.'),
    Paragraph('It\'s like a lifetime contract — the contract that makes your life more purposeful and meaningful.'),
    Highlight('The shahada isn\'t "done" the moment it\'s spoken. It\'s the starting point. The journey has only just begun.'),
    Subheading('💡 Why Is This the FIRST Pillar?'),
    Paragraph('Because without the shahada, the other 4 pillars have no foundation. Salat without believing in Allah? Just empty movements. Zakat without commitment? Just throwing money away. Fasting without Tawhid? Just a diet. Hajj without faith? Just a trip.'),
    Paragraph('The shahada is the foundation — the thing that gives all worship meaning, and makes you a Muslim.'),
    Cta('Done reading! Quiz time has arrived. 🎯'),
  ],
  'rukun_2.3': <ArticleBlock>[
    Heading('Why Fasting in Ramadan?'),
    Paragraph('Every year, Muslims around the world stop eating and drinking from dawn to sunset for a full month. Sounds heavy? It is. But there\'s a strong reason behind it.'),
    Subheading('🧘 Spiritual Benefit: Self-Control Training'),
    Paragraph('The core of fasting isn\'t "holding back from food." The core: training self-control. Want to eat? Hold back. Want to get angry? Hold back. Want to gossip? Hold back.'),
    Paragraph('Fasting is the soul\'s gym. If you can control your most basic desire (eating), you can also control more complex things.'),
    Highlight('Fasting isn\'t about "not eating." It\'s about: "who holds the controls — the desires, or you?"'),
    Subheading('🤝 Empathy for the Less Fortunate'),
    Paragraph('Have you ever felt real hunger? Not "skipped breakfast," but truly not eating all day. Fasting lets you feel what people who can\'t eat every day feel. From there, empathy emerges — and the drive to share.'),
    Subheading('🏥 Health Benefits (In General)'),
    Paragraph('Many studies show intermittent fasting (whose pattern resembles Ramadan fasting) has generally positive effects on the body. But that\'s not a medical claim — everyone\'s condition differs. What\'s certain: Ramadan fasting was designed by Allah, and Allah knows best what\'s good for His servants.'),
    Subheading('📋 Who Must Fast?'),
    Paragraph('Every Muslim who has come of age and is healthy must fast. But there are concessions for those unable:'),
    Paragraph('• The sick may skip fasting, but must make it up (qadha) after recovering.'),
    Paragraph('• The pregnant/breastfeeding may skip if worried about the baby — then qadha or fidyah.'),
    Paragraph('• The elderly/permanently unable aren\'t required to fast; paying fidyah suffices (feeding a poor person per day).'),
    Highlight('Islam is flexible. There are rules, but there are concessions. Nothing is forced beyond one\'s capacity.'),
    Subheading('💡 Conclusion'),
    Paragraph('Ramadan fasting isn\'t a punishment — it\'s training. Training discipline, empathy, and dependence on Allah. One full month that makes the remaining eleven more meaningful.'),
    Cta('Done reading! Quiz time. 🎯'),
  ],
  'rukun_2.4': <ArticleBlock>[
    Heading('Zakat: Why Share?'),
    Paragraph('You heard about zakat in the overview of the Pillars of Islam. Now let\'s dig deeper: why must you share from the wealth you worked so hard to earn?'),
    Subheading('🧹 Zakat = Wealth Purifier'),
    Paragraph('The word "zakat" itself means "pure" and "growth." The concept: the wealth you own isn\'t 100% yours. Others have a right to it — those in need, the less fortunate. By giving zakat, you "cleanse" your wealth from their rightful share.'),
    Paragraph('Imagine: you have a glass of water constantly being refilled. If it\'s never shared, the glass overflows and spills. Zakat keeps the flow moving — you receive, you share, and your wealth becomes more blessed.'),
    Highlight('Zakat isn\'t "throwing money away." It\'s an investment in the hereafter and a purification of wealth in this world.'),
    Subheading('📊 Zakat vs Infaq vs Sadaqah'),
    Paragraph('All three involve sharing, but their rules differ:'),
    Paragraph('• Zakat — OBLIGATORY. There\'s a nisab (minimum wealth threshold) and haul (owned for a full year). Its rate: 2.5% of wealth. Eight categories (asnaf) are entitled to receive it.'),
    Paragraph('• Infaq — SUNNAH. Sharing from wealth with no percentage limit. Any time, any amount, to anyone.'),
    Paragraph('• Sadaqah — SUNNAH. Broader than infaq — not only money. Even a smile is sadaqah. Helping someone, sharing knowledge, even removing a thorn from the road — that\'s sadaqah.'),
    Subheading('🌍 Social Impact: Reducing Inequality'),
    Paragraph('Zakat is a unique wealth-distribution system. Those with more → give 2.5% → those in need get help. If everyone obligated paid zakat, social inequality could shrink significantly.'),
    Paragraph('This isn\'t Western-style socialism; it\'s a system from Allah. And the difference: the MOTIVE of zakat is love, not coercion. You give because you believe it\'s their right, and because you care about your own wealth (you want it pure and blessed).'),
    Highlight('Zakat: one system that purifies your wealth WHILE helping your fellow humans. Win-win.'),
    Subheading('💡 Closing'),
    Paragraph('Zakat isn\'t a burden — it\'s someone else\'s right entrusted within your wealth. And when you give, what you "purify" isn\'t just your wealth, but also your heart.'),
    Cta('Now you understand zakat! Quiz time. 🎯'),
  ],
  'rukun_2.5': <ArticleBlock>[
    Heading('Hajj: The Once-in-a-Lifetime Journey'),
    Paragraph('This is the last pillar. And possibly the most "wow," because you have to actually travel to one place on the other side of the world, together with millions of people from across the planet.'),
    Subheading('🕋 What Is Hajj?'),
    Paragraph('Hajj is the pilgrimage worship to Makkah, Saudi Arabia. It\'s obligatory once in a lifetime for Muslims who are ABLE (physically and financially). Not able yet? It\'s not obligatory. Simple.'),
    Subheading('👕 Ihram: Everyone Equal'),
    Paragraph('What makes hajj different from other worship: everyone wears the SAME clothing. White, unstitched, no brands, no logos. It\'s called ihram.'),
    Paragraph('Imagine: a president, a laborer, a doctor, an ojek driver, an entrepreneur — all wearing the same clothes. Nobody can flaunt wealth. Nobody can flaunt rank. Before Allah, EVERYONE IS EQUAL.'),
    Highlight('Ihram is a reminder: in Allah\'s sight, what distinguishes you isn\'t wealth or position, but your taqwa.'),
    Subheading('⚖️ The Symbolic Meaning of Hajj'),
