    QuizQuestion(question: 'What\'s the difference between validity conditions and pillars of prayer?', options: <String>['Validity conditions apply only to imam; pillars to every follower', 'Validity conditions are fulfilled BEFORE prayer; pillars within it', 'Pillars may be dropped if forgotten; validity conditions can never be ignored', 'Validity conditions for obligatory prayer, pillars only for the sunnah'], correctIndex: 1, explanation: 'Validity conditions are things outside prayer (purity, aurat, qibla, time). Pillars are things inside it (intention, takbir, ruku, sujud, etc).'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'A man\'s aurat during prayer is...', options: <String>['The whole body except the face and both palms', 'From shoulders down to the knees including both arms', 'From navel to knees', 'Free as long as the clothing is neat, clean, and opaque'], correctIndex: 2, explanation: 'Men: navel to knees must be covered. Women: the whole body except face and palms.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'How many prostrations (sujud) are in one rakaat?', options: <String>['2 times', '1 time', '3 times', '4 times'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Every rakaat has 2 sujud. So a 2-rakaat prayer = 4 sujud, a 4-rakaat prayer = 8 sujud.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'What marks the START of the prayer?', options: <String>['Standing upright with the body facing the qibla', 'Reciting the prayer\'s intention silently with focus', 'Reciting Surat Al-Fatihah from its first verse', 'Takbiratul ihram — "Allahu Akbar" while raising the hands'], correctIndex: 3, explanation: 'Takbiratul ihram = the first \'Allahu Akbar\' with raised hands. That is the official start of the prayer.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'Praying outside the designated time — is it valid?', options: <String>['Valid, since what matters most is the intention in the heart', 'Valid as long as the lateness hasn\'t passed the next prayer time', 'Invalid — being within the time is one of the validity conditions', 'Valid if performed in congregation with an imam at the mosque'], correctIndex: 2, explanation: 'Being within the time is a validity condition. Praying outside it = invalid; it must be redone in the right time.'),
  ],
  'praktik_3.3': <QuizQuestion>[
    QuizQuestion(question: 'How many rakaat is Fajr prayer?', options: <String>['2 rakaat', '3 rakaat', '4 rakaat', '5 rakaat'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Fajr = 2 rakaat. Maghrib = 3. Dhuhr, Asr, Isha = 4. Start with Fajr if you\'re still learning.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'After Al-Fatihah in rakaat 1, what comes next?', options: <String>['The opening dua (iftitah) as the opener of the first rakaat\'s recitation', 'Going straight to ruku while saying Allahu Akbar', 'The two sentences of shahada before the next movement', 'A short surah — beginners are recommended to recite Al-Ikhlas'], correctIndex: 3, explanation: 'After Al-Fatihah, recite a short surah. Al-Ikhlas is recommended for beginners — short and easy to memorize.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'The correct ruku position is...', options: <String>['Bowing deeply until the forehead almost touches the knees', 'Hands gripping knees, back straight, gaze at the feet', 'Slight bow with both arms straight down', 'Hands on thighs, body upright facing the qibla'], correctIndex: 1, explanation: 'Ruku: bow, hands gripping the knees, back straight. Gaze at the feet, not ahead.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'In rakaat 3 and 4, what is recited?', options: <String>['Al-Fatihah plus a short surah, like in the previous rakaats', 'A short surah only, no Al-Fatihah, to finish faster', 'Only Al-Fatihah, with no added short surah', 'The first tasyahud recitation before standing fully'], correctIndex: 2, explanation: 'In rakaats 3 and 4, only Al-Fatihah is recited. No short surah added.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'The salam to the right and left means the prayer is...', options: <String>['Moving from an even rakaat into an odd rakaat', 'Entering the segment of additional duas before dhikr', 'Currently paying respect to the imam and congregation', 'FINISHED — this is the official sign that the prayer has ended'], correctIndex: 3, explanation: 'Salam = the sign the prayer is complete. Turn right saying \'Assalamu\'alaikum warahmatullah\', then to the left.'),
  ],
  'praktik_3.4': <QuizQuestion>[
    QuizQuestion(question: 'When is Al-Fatihah recited during prayer?', options: <String>['Only in the first rakaat, since the others follow it', 'In the first and last rakaat, as opener and closer', 'It may be skipped if the imam has recited it aloud', 'In every rakaat — obligatory; without it the rakaat is void'], correctIndex: 3, explanation: 'Al-Fatihah must be recited in EVERY rakaat, whether leading or praying alone. Without Al-Fatihah the rakaat doesn\'t count.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'The recitations of ruku and sujud are...', options: <String>['Exactly the same — both recite Subhana Rabbyal Adhim', 'Different — ruku recites \'Adhim (the Great), sujud recites A\'la (the Most High)', 'Freely choosing any dhikr as long as it\'s said three times', 'Repeating \'Allahu Akbar\' three times is enough'], correctIndex: 1, explanation: 'Ruku = Subhana Rabbyal \'Adhim wa bihamdih (Glory to my Lord the Greatest). Sujud = Subhana Rabbyal A\'la wa bihamdih (Glory to my Lord the Most High).'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'What is added in the final tasyahud compared with the first?', options: <String>['An added Al-Fatihah recitation closing the prayer series', 'The iftitah dua recited at the prayer\'s beginning', 'The Ibrahimiyah salawat for Prophet Muhammad ﷺ', 'Nothing — only the sitting position changes'], correctIndex: 2, explanation: 'The final tasyahud = first tasyahud + Ibrahimiyah salawat. Reciting the salawat is obligatory there.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'The recitation while sitting between the two prostrations asks for what?', options: <String>['Forgiveness, mercy, sufficiency, raised rank, pardon, and sustenance', 'Protection from the grave\'s torment and end-times fitnah', 'Salvation in this world and the next for parents and family', 'Upright guidance like the one in Surat Al-Fatihah'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Allahummaghfirli (forgiveness), warhamni (mercy), wajburni (sufficiency), warfa\'ni (raised rank), wa\'fu \'anni (pardon), warzuqni (sustenance).'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'What is this module (the cheat-sheet) useful for?', options: <String>['Mandatory material to memorize entirely in one day', 'A quick reference — all prayer recitations in one place', 'A substitute for actual prayer practice when there\'s no time', 'Material specifically for those becoming a congregational imam'], correctIndex: 1, explanation: 'Cheat-sheet = quick reference. Bookmark this module — you\'ll return here often while learning to pray.'),
  ],
  'praktik_3.5': <QuizQuestion>[
    QuizQuestion(question: 'How many rakaat is Fajr prayer?', options: <String>['4', '3', '2', '5'], correctIndex: 2, explanation: 'Fajr = 2 rakaat. The obligatory prayer with the fewest rakaat.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'The obligatory prayer with 3 rakaat is...', options: <String>['Maghrib', 'Fajr', 'Isha', 'Dhuhr'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Maghrib = 3 rakaat. The only obligatory prayer with 3. Fajr is 2, the rest are 4.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'How many total rakaat are in the obligatory prayers in one day?', options: <String>['20', '15', '19', '17'], correctIndex: 3, explanation: '2 + 4 + 4 + 3 + 4 = 17 rakaat. Spread across the day, it isn\'t much.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'When does Asr time begin?', options: <String>['When the sun sits exactly over our heads', 'When an object\'s shadow equals the object\'s length', 'When the sky starts reddening at the western horizon', 'When an object\'s shadow is twice its length'], correctIndex: 1, explanation: 'Asr starts when an object\'s shadow equals its length (around 15:00).'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'Why is Fajr only 2 rakaat?', options: <String>['Because its window is short — from dawn to sunrise', 'Because Allah gives ease to those who just woke up', 'Because the Prophet ﷺ once shortened (qashar) Fajr while traveling', 'Because its rakaat are completed by the qabliyah voluntary prayers'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Fajr is only 2 rakaat because its window is short. The rakaa count was set by the Prophet ﷺ based on revelation.'),
  ],
  'praktik_3.6': <QuizQuestion>[
    QuizQuestion(question: 'If you forget which rakaat you\'re at, the solution is...', options: <String>['Cancel the prayer and restart from takbiratul ihram', 'Take the smaller count, add sujud sahwi after the salam', 'Take the larger count so the rakaat never comes up short', 'Follow your best guess then go straight to salam'], correctIndex: 1, explanation: 'Take the smaller count, add sujud sahwi after the salam. Forgetting is human — no panic.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'Can\'t memorize any short surah besides Al-Fatihah — what now?', options: <String>['Postpone the prayer until you know at least three short surahs', 'Read its Indonesian translation as a substitute', 'No problem — Al-Fatihah alone suffices; the rest follows later', 'Repeat Al-Fatihah twice in every rakaat'], correctIndex: 2, explanation: 'Al-Fatihah is what\'s obligatory. The short surah is an addition — any memorized surah is fine. Focus on Al-Fatihah first.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'Qashar prayer means...', options: <String>['Shortening a 4-rakaat prayer into 2, specifically for travelers', 'Speeding up prayer movements because time is tight', 'Combining two obligatory prayers into one travel time', 'Repeating a missed prayer at the next prayer time'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Qashar = shortening 4 rakaat into 2. Specifically for musafir (long trips). Islam doesn\'t overburden.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'May the pillars\' recitations be in Indonesian?', options: <String>['Yes — Allah understands every language of His servants', 'Yes for beginners, until they memorize the Arabic', 'It depends on each mosque\'s and imam\'s local habit', 'No — the pillars must be Arabic; additional duas are free in any language'], correctIndex: 3, explanation: 'Pillars = Arabic (obligatory — the Quran is in Arabic and prayer was standardized by the Prophet). Additional duas = any language.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'Prayer still imperfect despite the effort — is it valid?', options: <String>['Not valid until all recitations are fluent and movements exact', 'Valid only when performed in congregation behind an imam', 'Valid — as long as the validity conditions are met and the pillars complete', 'Half valid — so the reward granted is also halved'], correctIndex: 2, explanation: 'An imperfect prayer beats no prayer at all. Allah knows you\'re still learning. The key: DON\'T STOP.'),
  ],
  'rukun_2.1': <QuizQuestion>[
    QuizQuestion(question: 'What does "Pillars of Islam" mean?', options: <String>['Five recommended voluntary deeds for the obedient Muslim', 'Five supporting columns that form a Muslim\'s life foundation', 'Five rules you can pick according to each person\'s ability', 'Five spiritual levels reached in sequence over a lifetime'], correctIndex: 1, explanation: '"Pillar" means a supporting column. All five are obligatory and complete one another; you can\'t pick and choose.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'What is the role of Salat in a Muslim\'s life?', options: <String>['A direct conversation with Allah — a standing appointment 5 times a day', 'A sin-erasing ritual performed only when feeling guilty', 'Body-stretching exercise at five different times of day', 'A social obligation so neighbors consider you a good Muslim'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Salat is your standing appointment with God — 5 times a day, not delegable, not skippable.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'Zakat differs from tax because...', options: <String>['Zakat\'s amount is far larger than the government tax', 'Zakat is paid only once a year, during Ramadan', 'Zakat is managed directly by the mosque with no set rules', 'Zakat is a purification of wealth — others have a right to your wealth'], correctIndex: 3, explanation: 'Zakat isn\'t a tax; it\'s a wealth purifier. The concept: your wealth isn\'t 100% yours — others hold a right in it.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'The goal of Ramadan fasting isn\'t just enduring hunger, but also...', options: <String>['Cutting food spending for a full month', 'Proving a Muslim\'s physical strength to others', 'Training discipline, empathy, and closeness to Allah', 'Carrying on a tradition inherited from our ancestors'], correctIndex: 2, explanation: 'Fasting trains discipline and empathy. It\'s not just abstaining from food, but also emotions, gossip, and negativity.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'When is Hajj obligatory?', options: <String>['Once in a lifetime, if physically and financially able', 'Every five years for those with steady income', 'Once in a lifetime for every Muslim without exception', 'Every year for those living near the city of Makkah'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Hajj is obligatory once in a lifetime if one is able. There, millions gather from around the world; there\'s no rich-poor distinction.'),
  ],
  'rukun_2.2': <QuizQuestion>[
    QuizQuestion(question: 'What does "Laa ilaaha illallah" mean?', options: <String>['Allah exists and created the entire universe', 'Nothing can rival Allah\'s power', 'Allah is Greater than anything that ever existed', 'There is no god worthy of worship except Allah'], correctIndex: 3, explanation: 'It\'s not merely saying "God exists," but "He alone is worthy of my worship and obedience." It\'s about life\'s priorities.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'What is the consequence of saying "Muhammadur Rasulullah"?', options: <String>['Memorizing every hadith narrated from him', 'Believing he\'s Allah\'s messenger AND committing to follow his teachings', 'Just remembering his merits every Mawlid commemoration', 'Making him the intermediary of every dua we make to Allah'], correctIndex: 1, explanation: 'If you truly believe he\'s God\'s messenger, the logical step is to follow his teachings — like trusting a mentor means taking his advice.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: '"Laa ilaaha illallah" in daily life means...', options: <String>['Allah is number one in life — not money or position', 'Reciting that sentence at least one hundred times every day', 'Rejecting every worldly affair and focusing only on ritual worship', 'Avoiding all people whose beliefs differ from ours'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Laa ilaaha illallah = life priority. Never fear or depend on anything more than Allah.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'Why is Shahada the FIRST pillar?', options: <String>['Because it\'s the shortest and easiest to practice', 'Because the Prophet ﷺ first spoke it while receiving revelation', 'Because without shahada the other four pillars have no foundation', 'Because the pillars are ordered from the lightest first'], correctIndex: 2, explanation: 'Salat without believing in Allah = empty motion. Zakat without commitment = wasted money. The shahada is the foundation giving worship its meaning.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'Is the shahada the end of the spiritual journey?', options: <String>['Yes — once spoken, a Muslim\'s main obligation is complete', 'No, it\'s the starting point; the journey has only just begun', 'Yes, the rest is just filling that isn\'t very important', 'It depends on how deep the speaker\'s understanding is'], correctIndex: 1, explanation: 'The shahada isn\'t the finish line — it\'s the starting line. A lifetime commitment that has just begun.'),
  ],
  'rukun_2.3': <QuizQuestion>[
    QuizQuestion(question: 'The core of Ramadan fasting isn\'t only enduring hunger, but...', options: <String>['Training self-control over emotions, desires, and urges', 'Detoxifying the body\'s toxins for a full month', 'Saving grocery money to prep for Eid day', 'Proving our obedience to family and neighbors'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Fasting is the soul\'s gym. If you can control the most basic desire (eating), you can control more complex things too.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'What\'s the connection between fasting and empathy?', options: <String>['Fasting makes us share leftover iftar food more often', 'Empathy comes because we break fast with family daily', 'Feeling real hunger makes us understand the plight of the poor', 'Fasting trains patience against annoying people'], correctIndex: 2, explanation: 'By feeling real hunger, you come to understand the situation of those who can\'t eat every day.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'If someone is sick, must they fast Ramadan?', options: <String>['They must fast fully — sickness isn\'t an excuse to miss', 'No obligation and no makeup since there\'s a valid excuse', 'Half-day fasting according to each person\'s physical ability', 'They may skip, but must make it up (qadha) after recovery'], correctIndex: 3, explanation: 'Islam is flexible. Sickness = may skip. But once recovered, making it up on other days is required.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'An elderly person permanently unable to fast — what\'s the solution?', options: <String>['Their fast is delegated to strong children or grandchildren', 'No obligation to fast; paying fidyah (feeding the poor) suffices', 'Fasting half a day, from dawn to noon only', 'Replacing it with abundant dhikr and dua'], correctIndex: 1, explanation: 'Elderly/permanently unable = no obligation, fidyah suffices. Islam doesn\'t force beyond capacity.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'Is Ramadan fasting a punishment or training?', options: <String>['A punishment — an atonement for sins made all year', 'Both, depending on how many sins the person commits', 'Training — it builds discipline, empathy, and dependence on Allah', 'An annual physical exam measuring one\'s strength of faith'], correctIndex: 2, explanation: 'Fasting is training, not punishment. One month that makes the other eleven more meaningful.'),
  ],
  'rukun_2.4': <QuizQuestion>[
    QuizQuestion(question: 'What does the word "zakat" mean?', options: <String>['Voluntary giving', 'Pure and growing', 'Wealth obligation', 'Sharing sustenance'], correctIndex: 1, explanation: 'Zakat means \'pure\' and \'growing.\' The concept: cleansing your wealth from others\' rights, and your wealth grows more blessed.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'What\'s the difference between zakat, infaq, and sadaqah?', options: <String>['Zakat for mosques, infaq for the poor, sadaqah free to anyone', 'Zakat uses money, infaq goods, sadaqah labor or services', 'Zakat is obligatory with set rules (2.5%, nisab, haul); infaq and sadaqah are sunnah', 'All three are obligatory; the difference is only the payment timing'], correctIndex: 2, explanation: 'Zakat = obligatory with strict rules (2.5%, nisab, haul). Infaq = sunnah, sharing wealth. Sadaqah = sunnah and broader — even a smile is sadaqah.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'Why can zakat reduce social inequality?', options: <String>['Because zakat forces the rich to live simply like everyone else', 'Because all zakat proceeds fund public facilities', 'Because zakat recipients must use the money as business capital', 'Those with more give 2.5% to those in need — wealth gets distributed'], correctIndex: 3, explanation: 'Zakat = Allah\'s wealth-distribution system. The able → give 2.5% → the needy get help. If everyone complies, inequality shrinks significantly.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'What is the right motivation for zakat?', options: <String>['Love — believing it\'s their right, and wanting your wealth pure and blessed', 'Fear that wealth will lose its blessing if not quickly given out', 'Expecting praise and respect from the surrounding community', 'Meeting the obligation so mosque officials won\'t collect it'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Zakat\'s motive is love, not coercion. You give because you believe it\'s their right and because you love your own wealth — you want it pure and blessed.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'Is sadaqah only about money?', options: <String>['Yes — it must be cash so its value and benefit are clear', 'Money or valuables — what matters is that the value can be counted', 'Yes, though for family it can become labor assistance', 'No — a smile, helping someone, even removing a thorn from the road is sadaqah'], correctIndex: 3, explanation: 'Sadaqah is broader than money. A smile, helping someone, sharing knowledge, even removing a thorn from the road — all of it is sadaqah.'),
  ],
  'rukun_2.5': <QuizQuestion>[
    QuizQuestion(question: 'When is hajj obligatory?', options: <String>['Once every year for every healthy-bodied Muslim', 'Once in a lifetime, if physically and financially able', 'Twice in a lifetime: when young and after retirement', 'Anytime as long as there\'s savings, with no other conditions'], correctIndex: 1, explanation: 'Hajj is obligatory once in a lifetime BUT only if able. If not able yet, there\'s no sin.'),
    QuizQuestion(question: 'Why do all pilgrims wear the same white clothing (ihram)?', options: <String>['Because white best endures the heat of the Arabian desert', 'So officials can easily tell pilgrims apart from locals', 'Following the clothing tradition of the Arabs before Islam', 'Symbolizing equality — before Allah all are equal, no wealth difference'], correctIndex: 3, explanation: 'Ihram = white clothing without brands. Presidents, laborers, entrepreneurs — all the same. What distinguishes in Allah\'s sight isn\'t wealth, but taqwa.'),
